<section class="cxx entity object">
<h2>ByteArrayFactory and storage</h2>
<p><code>Arcadia_ByteArrayFactory_createImmutable</code> wraps an immutable
<code>Arcadia_RuntimeByteArray</code> without copying and keeps the runtime value
reachable through GC traversal. The immutable runtime value remains distinct
from the object-level <code>Arcadia_ByteArray</code>.</p>
<p><code>Arcadia_ByteArrayFactory_createMutable</code> creates mutable,
fixed-size contiguous native storage; <code>createResizable</code> creates mutable,
resizable contiguous native storage. Both zero-initialize their requested size.
<code>copyMutable</code> and <code>copyResizable</code> make independent copies of
contiguous ByteArray sources. <code>copyImmutable</code> creates a separate
immutable runtime-value-backed snapshot; it never aliases the source, even if
its capability queries report immutable. Copies use a single native bulk-copy
for contiguous sources and logical <code>getAt</code> reads for non-contiguous
sources. Non-contiguous copying may be slower, but requires no native pointer
from the source. Both paths retain the source during copying; destination
storage remains independently owned. Zero-length immutable snapshots are
supported even when a source has no native byte pointer.</p>
<p><code>Arcadia_ByteArrayFactory_borrowReadOnly</code> and
<code>Arcadia_ByteArrayFactory_borrowMutable</code> create fixed-size contiguous
views over existing native C memory without copying or allocating storage.
They preserve the caller's pointer and never deallocate it. The caller MUST
keep that memory alive and valid until the ByteArray object is no longer used.
A managed ByteArray reference does not keep external memory alive. The mutable
variant permits in-place writes, while the read-only variant rejects mutation;
both reject resizing. A nonempty view requires a non-null pointer; an empty
view may use null. Caller-owned memory must not be relocated while borrowed,
and only native C callers may pass the pointer—never PDL or Arcadia.Value.</p>
<p>The factory-owned writable storage is deallocated with its object.
<code>Arcadia_ByteArray_getMutableBytes</code> is a native-C-only writable
borrow for mutable contiguous storage; immutable wrappers and read-only slice
views reject this request with <code>Arcadia_Status_OperationInvalid</code>.
The caller may write any byte in the current logical range without per-byte
virtual dispatch. The caller must not write beyond that range.
A borrow from <code>Arcadia_ByteArray_getBytes</code> is native C-only and valid only while
the underlying object and its storage are alive; resizing can relocate or
invalidate a borrowed pointer. The returned pointer must not be stored in
PDL values. Clients must root managed objects while borrowing their storage.
A borrow is invalidated by any operation that may relocate storage; reacquire
the pointer after resizing. Never cache mutable pointers across a resize.</p>
<p>Fixed-size mutable storage supports <code>setAt</code> but rejects
<code>resize</code> with <code>Arcadia_Status_OperationInvalid</code> even when
the requested size equals the current size. Mutable writes outside the current
bounds raise <code>Arcadia_Status_ArgumentValueInvalid</code>. Growable storage
zeroes newly exposed elements, including after shrinking and regrowing.</p>
<p><code>Arcadia_ByteArray</code> is the common capability-aware object
abstraction. The concrete <code>ByteArrayNativeImpl</code> is the only
factory-created owned mutable allocation, serving fixed-size and resizable
variants. <code>ByteArrayBorrowedImpl</code> is the non-owning fixed-size
adapter for external native memory; it never frees the caller's storage.
<code>ByteArrayDefaultImpl</code> is retained as the zero-copy wrapper around
a built-in immutable <code>RuntimeByteArray</code> value, which remains a
distinct runtime value and retains its existing GC visitation.
<code>ByteArraySliceImpl</code> is retained as a factory-created read-only
view, not an independent allocation. Concrete type registration remains for
compatibility, but general callers should construct arrays via the factory.</p>
<p>A slice is fixed-size and immutable through its interface, regardless of
its backing array's mutability. Its contiguity capability is inherited from
the backing array; native <code>getBytes</code> on a non-contiguous slice
raises <code>Arcadia_Status_OperationInvalid</code>, while indexed reads
work. A slice keeps its backing object reachable through GC visitation.
If the backing shrinks beyond the slice's original range, indexed and
native-pointer reads fail with <code>Arcadia_Status_ArgumentValueInvalid</code>
until the original range becomes valid again. Resizing a backing array
invalidates any previously borrowed pointer, even when the slice remains
logically valid.</p>
<p><code>ByteArrayBuilder</code> remains a compatibility facade over a
factory-created resizable native ByteArray; it has no second owned byte
allocation. Its native size/capacity/pointer fields mirror the underlying
storage, including after resizing and swapping. Bulk insertion permits
self-insertion across its existing logical range, including the insertion
point; pointers into unused reserved capacity are rejected before a possible
reallocation.</p>
<p><code>ByteArray.ByteReader</code>, <code>ByteArrayBuilder.ByteReader</code>
and <code>RuntimeByteArray.ByteReader</code> remain registered because
production text, filesystem, scanning and parsing consumers rely on their
existing public identities. The builder reader accesses the same underlying
ByteArray object as the builder facade. Each reader tracks an indexed cursor,
reports <code>hasValue</code> against current source length, and raises
<code>Arcadia_Status_OperationInvalid</code> when asked to read or advance
at end-of-input. Resizable sources may re-expose an exhausted cursor when
grown. Reader objects retain their source through GC visitation. This is
intentional compatibility, not duplicate native storage.</p>
</section>
